# npm refuses a `lockfileVersion` it does not recognise only in the sense that it ignores
# it; the setting below is the one that actually changes behaviour, and it is a security
# setting, not a preference. See docs/security-baseline.md ("Lockfile integrity").
#
# WHAT THIS DOES
# package-lock.json records a `resolved` URL for every package, and npm fetches from it.
# The `replace-registry-host` default, `npmjs`, rewrites that host only when it already
# reads `registry.npmjs.org` -- so a lockfile edited to point at another host is obeyed
# exactly as written. Measured on npm 10.9.8 with an empty cache: repointing all 308
# `resolved` URLs produced 233 requests to the substituted host.
#
# `always` rewrites every `resolved` host to the configured registry, so the lockfile cannot
# redirect where bytes are downloaded from. The integrity hash was still the last line of
# defence before this; now it is not the only one.
#
# DO NOT COPY THIS BLINDLY
# `always` also rewrites URLs that legitimately point elsewhere -- GitHub Packages, a
# private Verdaccio or JFrog proxy. In those setups the install fails with a 404 for every
# private package. Fails loudly, which is the right failure mode, but it is a failure.
# A repository with private scoped packages should point `registry=` at a proxy that serves
# both, so there is exactly one host to rewrite to.
#
# Intentionally NOT set here: `registry=`. Pointing this repository at a mirror would change
# what is downloaded, which belongs in a decision, not a dotfile added as a side effect.
replace-registry-host=always
